  "type": "numerical",
  "level": 2,
  "question": {
    "text": "The potential energy of a particle of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> in motion along the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math>-axis is given by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">U</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><mi>cos</mi><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math>. The time period of the particle for small oscillation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mi>&#952;</mi><mo>&#8771;</mo><mi>&#952;</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></mfrac></mfenced><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi></math> is _____ .",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "2",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Given here, potential energy,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><mi>cos</mi><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math></p><p>Using the relation between conservative force and potential energy,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>d</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>d</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>.</p><p>We have,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn><mfenced><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>sin</mi><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>16</mn><mi>sin</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p>For small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#952;</mi></math>,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>sin</mi><mi>&#952;</mi><mo>&#8776;</mo><mi>&#952;</mi></math>.</p><p>Acceleration of particle is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>64</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>64</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>16</mn><mi>x</mi></math></p><p>As the oscillations are simple harmonic in nature, so&nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>&#969;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#8658;</mo><msup><mi>&#969;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>&#969;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>rad</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></p><p>Now, time period of oscillation is<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mi>&#969;</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>.</p><p>Thus, the value of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math>.</p></blockquote>",
    "image": null
  },
